{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fjif9m3ntSfFJmFogFsxje","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tsource_url: \"https://github.com/cncf/toc/blob/main/DEFINITION.md\"\n3\tfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T22:30:00-0400\"\n4\tauthority: \"Cloud Native Computing Foundation Technical Oversight Committee\"\n5\tobjectives_covered: [\"D4 Cloud Native Ecosystem and Principles\"]\n6\tconcepts_covered: [\"cloud-native-definition\", \"loosely-coupled\", \"declarative-apis\", \"immutable-infrastructure\", \"microservices\", \"service-mesh\", \"serverless\"]\n7\t---\n8\t# CNCF Cloud Native Definition v1.1 (github.com/cncf/toc/blob/main/DEFINITION.md)\n9\t\n10\tCloud native practices empower organizations to develop, build, and deploy workloads in computing environments (public, private, hybrid cloud) to meet their organizational needs at scale in a programmatic and repeatable manner. It is characterized by loosely coupled systems that interoperate in a manner that is secure, resilient, manageable, sustainable, and observable.\n11\t\n12\tCloud native technologies and architectures typically consist of some combination of containers, service meshes, multi-tenancy, microservices, immutable infrastructure, serverless, and declarative APIs — this list is non-exhaustive.\n13\t\n14\tThese techniques enable loosely coupled systems that are resilient, manageable, and observable. Combined with robust automation, they allow engineers to make high-impact changes frequently and predictably with minimal toil and clear separation of concerns.\n15\t\n16\tThe Cloud Native Computing Foundation seeks to drive adoption of this paradigm by fostering and sustaining an ecosystem of open source, vendor-neutral projects. We democratize state-of-the-art patterns to make these innovations accessible for everyone.\n17\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bdbd2677-ed1a-46d2-8a51-553b92d3c7e0","uuid":"f30c5edf-6f57-49b2-ae64-0e3632fd1c71","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:23:41.819Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/sources/cncf-cloud-native-definition-2026-08-23.md","content":"---\nsource_url: \"https://github.com/cncf/toc/blob/main/DEFINITION.md\"\nfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T22:30:00-0400\"\nauthority: \"Cloud Native Computing Foundation Technical Oversight Committee\"\nobjectives_covered: [\"D4 Cloud Native Ecosystem and Principles\"]\nconcepts_covered: [\"cloud-native-definition\", \"loosely-coupled\", \"declarative-apis\", \"immutable-infrastructure\", \"microservices\", \"service-mesh\", \"serverless\"]\n---\n# CNCF Cloud Native Definition v1.1 (github.com/cncf/toc/blob/main/DEFINITION.md)\n\nCloud native practices empower organizations to develop, build, and deploy workloads in computing environments (public, private, hybrid cloud) to meet their organizational needs at scale in a programmatic and repeatable manner. It is characterized by loosely coupled systems that interoperate in a manner that is secure, resilient, manageable, sustainable, and observable.\n\nCloud native technologies and architectures typically consist of some combination of containers, service meshes, multi-tenancy, microservices, immutable infrastructure, serverless, and declarative APIs — this list is non-exhaustive.\n\nThese techniques enable loosely coupled systems that are resilient, manageable, and observable. Combined with robust automation, they allow engineers to make high-impact changes frequently and predictably with minimal toil and clear separation of concerns.\n\nThe Cloud Native Computing Foundation seeks to drive adoption of this paradigm by fostering and sustaining an ecosystem of open source, vendor-neutral projects. We democratize state-of-the-art patterns to make these innovations accessible for everyone.\n","numLines":17,"startLine":1,"totalLines":17}}}
